        "content": "<p>Destructor17 opened <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14803\">PR #14803</a> from <code>webrogue-runtime:android-openat2-api-gate</code> to <code>bytecodealliance:main</code>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Closes <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/issues/14496\">https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/issues/14496</a></p>\n<p>Android's Seccomp policy prior API level 37 is to raise a fatal signal on <code>openat2</code> syscall instead of returning <code>ENOSYS</code>, and it makes Wasmtime crash.<br>\nRecently released API level 37 finally allowed <code>openat2</code> (see <a href=\"https://android.googlesource.com/platform/bionic/+/refs/heads/android17-release/libc/SECCOMP_ALLOWLIST_COMMON.TXT\">SECCOMP_ALLOWLIST_COMMON.TXT</a>, it now includes <code>openat2</code>). </p>\n<p>Device's API level needs check at runtime. I did my best to avoid dependency on <code>ndk</code> crate, so <code>dlsym</code> and some unsafe code is used to detect API level. <code>CHECKED</code> flag is inspired by <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/blob/b140c545f5e5523fed8ee42a6166b1fa04d39b31/crates/wasi/src/filesystem/primitives/unix/freebsd.rs#L14\">crates/wasi/src/filesystem/primitives/unix/freebsd.rs</a>.</p>\n<p>Tested by running tests from <code>wasmtime-wasi</code> crate on real Android 13 (API level 33) device and x64 emulator of Android 17 (API level 37). Now all tests pass, with the exception of tests that can't run because Android's SELinux disallows hard links. Testing is done by AI and involved building Rust code using <code>cargo-ndk</code>, building an Android Gradle project just because running binary directly using <code>adb shell</code> applies slightly different Seccomp rules, and other dirty hacks, so I think I shouldn't include such test script. Some manual testing is done too.<br>\n</p>\n</blockquote>",
